          "secNumber": "8.2",
          "heading": "A method is an argument, not just a procedure",
          "content": "The topics in this unit share a concern with rights, permissions and consequences. A sound method specifies what is being compared and explains why the measured response says something about the broader biological system. A result is interpretable only at the level supported by assignment, selection, calibration and analysis. The study may produce exact digital values while still leaving ecological or mechanistic uncertainty that cannot be converted into additional decimal places. In this unit, map decisions made before measurement separately from choices introduced while inspecting results. An acceptable alternative design may target a different estimand; that can be a strength when the alternatives are made visible.\n\n### Practice before computation\n\nGovernance workshop: map stakeholders, data sensitivities, permitted uses and accountable roles for a hypothetical biological study. Identify what would require independent ethics review, animal welfare oversight, laboratory risk assessment or field permits. Draft a decision brief giving benefits, uncertainty and a monitoring plan, and ensure its recommendations remain within the evidence.\n\n### The running scenario\n\nFictional investigation: A restoration team considers a new habitat intervention in a community-managed coastal area. Construct a governance plan for observations, consultation, consent where relevant, restricted coordinates, impact metrics and transparent revision triggers.\n\n### Responsibility and scope\n\nEthical boundary: hypothetical class exercises are not operational instructions or permissions for human, animal, genetic or hazardous-agent research.",
